--TEST--
Spec § 4: `Instant::since` returns a distance of exactly the longest `Duration` either way, and throws one nanosecond past it
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Time\Instant::since
// 9223372037 whole seconds do not fit in an `int` of nanoseconds, and the part
// of a second brings each distance back to exactly the end of the range. A
// member that multiplies the seconds out first throws on both pairs.
var $late = Core\Time::fromIso("2262-04-11T23:47:17Z");
echo $late->since(Core\Time::fromIso("1970-01-01T00:00:00.145224193Z"))->toNanoseconds() as string, "|\n";
echo Core\Time::fromIso("1970-01-01T00:00:00.145224192Z")->since($late)->toNanoseconds() as string, "|\n";

// One nanosecond past either end throws, naming the member.
try {
    echo $late->since(Core\Time::fromIso("1970-01-01T00:00:00.145224192Z"))->toNanoseconds() as string, "|\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "|\n";
}
try {
    echo Core\Time::fromIso("1970-01-01T00:00:00.145224191Z")->since($late)->toNanoseconds() as string, "|\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "|\n";
}
--EXPECT--
9223372036854775807|
-9223372036854775808|
Core\Time\Instant::since(): the two instants are further apart than a `Duration` can hold|
Core\Time\Instant::since(): the two instants are further apart than a `Duration` can hold|
